CREATED:20260913T201324Z
DESCRIPTION:Photographer and Duke MFA|EDA alumna Cassandra Klos explores h
 umanity's relationship to space\, scientific exploration\, and the possib
 ility of life beyond Earth in "Cosmic Origins\," an exhibition on view at
  CDS October 22 - January 4\, 2026.\n\nGALLERY HOURS\n\nMonday - Thursday
 : 9 am - 5 pm.\nFriday: 9 am - 4 pm.\n**By appointment only in December. 
 Please email docstudies@duke.edu**\n\nBringing together photographs\, art
 ifacts\, and ephemera from simulated space missions\, the exhibition feat
 ures work from Klos's long-term project "Mars on Earth\," which documents
  her experiences as an analog astronaut participating in Earth-based simu
 lations designed to replicate the conditions\, challenges\, and isolation
  of space exploration.\n\nWith pressurized spacesuits\, freeze-dried food
 \, robotic companions\, and Mission Control waiting back at "home\," Klos
 's photographs visualize what life beyond Earth might look and feel like 
 for the first cosmic explorers. The exhibition also includes spacesuits a
 nd materials from Klos's fieldwork\, as well as work originally produced 
 for National Geographic that will be exhibited together in the CDS Kreps 
 Gallery for the first time.\n\nBlurring the boundaries between documentar
 y evidence and speculative imagination\, "Cosmic Origins" invites visitor
 s to consider not only how humans might live on the Moon or Mars\, but al
 so what space exploration can teach us about life on Earth.\n\nMore info 
 about the exhibition: https://documentarystudies.duke.edu/upcoming-exhibi
 tions. Organized by CDS in collaboration with Duke University's MFA|EDA p
 rogram and the Duke SPACE Initiative\, with support from Duke's Office of
  Interdisciplinary Programs.
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270104T050000
DTSTAMP:20261106T010703Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261022T090000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261106T010703Z
