CREATED:20261104T231737Z
DESCRIPTION:9:00 - 10:00: Elijah Sheridan (Cornell)\, "Advances in Calabi-
 Yau Geometry for String Phenomenology"\nAbstract: String phenomenology ro
 utinely reduces to problems in Calabi-Yau (CY) geometry. This motivates b
 oth inquiry into the general structure of the set of CY varieties - const
 raining the space of UV-complete theories - and development of efficient 
 methods for the explicit computations crucial for building the IR EFT of 
 any fixed compactification. In this direction\, I will present progress i
 n two areas of CY geometry. \n\n10:15 - 11:15: Anthony Ashmore (Skidmore)
 \, "Supersymmetric deformations\, RG flows and their supergravity duals"\
 nAbstract: It is a long-standing problem to find the explicit string back
 grounds dual to generic N=1 deformations of N=4 super Yang-Mills. We appr
 oach this problem using generalised geometry\, where supersymmetric AdS b
 ackgrounds are described by a pair of geometric structures satisfying alg
 ebraic and differential conditions. \n\n1:15 - 2:15: Weihong Xu (Emory)\,
  "Counting curves in flag varieties"\nAbstract: I will discuss a mathemat
 ical way to count maps satisfying incidence conditions from a fixed smoot
 h curve to a (type A) partial flag variety--we use the Hyperquot scheme t
 o compactify the space of maps and perform a virtual intersection-theoret
 ic calculation. The resulting formula matches a formula for a correlation
  function in supersymmetric gauge theory. Joint with Riccardo Ontani and 
 Shubham Sinha.\n\n2:30 - 3:30: Mithat Unsal (NCSU)\n\n3:45 - 4:15: James 
 Gray (VT)\, "GLSMs for non-polynomial deformations and gCICYs"\nAbstract:
  While descriptions of complete intersections of polynomial defining equa
 tions have long been understood in the context of GLSMs\, a full treatmen
 t of non-polynomial complex structure deformations has been absent from t
 he literature. Likewise\, the somewhat more exotic generalized complete i
 ntersection Calabi-Yau construction has resisted a GLSM description. In t
 his brief talk we describe how these omissions can be resolved using comp
 letely standard GLSMs with polynomial superpotentials. The non-polynomial
  structure seen in the geometric constructions only arises in the associa
 ted GLSM upon restriction to the vacuum space of the theory. This feature
  enables the construction to avoid singularities in field space that coul
 d have arisen if non-polynomial structure were incorporated directly into
  the GLSM superpotential.
DURATION:PT7H15M
DTSTAMP:20261104T231737Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261107T090000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261104T231737Z
