CREATED:20261014T184113Z
DESCRIPTION:Drawing on fieldwork conducted in a gangxu development at the 
 urban periphery of east Nanjing from 2013 to 2015\, this talk argues that
  the concept of "inflexible demand" works as an empty signifier bridging 
 divergent value-making projects that are constitutive of China's housing 
 market. It argues that gangxu is a hegemonic concept through which people
  contest inclusion and exclusion in relation to the city in contemporary 
 China. The talk is developed from a new book titled "Anxious Homes: Infle
 xible Demand and China's Housing Market."\n\nAbout the speaker:\nMengqi W
 ang is an Assistant Professor of Anthropology at Duke Kunshan University 
 (DKU). Her research focus is the intersection among economic anthropology
 \, urban studies\, and critical infrastructure studies. She is especially
  interested in how the home-as an object of attachment\, a condition of s
 ocial reproduction\, and a residential property-mediate capital accumulat
 ion and urban governance in contemporary China. Her teaching interests at
  DKU include Cultural Anthropology\, Social Science\, and Cultures and So
 cieties.\n\nShe is the author of an ethnographic monograph\, "Anxious Hom
 es: Inflexible Demand and China's Housing Market" (Cornell University Pre
 ss\, 2026)\, and has had papers published in leading academic journals in
 cluding Urban Studies\, Positions: Asia Critique\, and the Journal of Cul
 tural Economy.
DURATION:PT1H30M
DTSTAMP:20261014T184113Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261109T133000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261014T184113Z
