CREATED:20261103T152604Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>We are pleased to invite you to the in-person lecture "A Re
 lative\, Not an Object: Glicéria Tupinambá\, the Tupinambá Mantle\, and H
 ãhãwpuá at the 2024 Venice Biennale\," presented by Prof. Camila Maroja(B
 randeis University).\n\n<p>Date: Monday\, November 9\nTime: 03:30 - 05:00
  PM\nLocation: Ahmadieh Family Lecture Hall | Smith Warehouse Bay 4\nLang
 uage: English\nFormat: In person only (this event will not be held virtua
 lly)\n\n<p>Abstract\nIn 2024\, the Brazilian Pavilion at the Venice Bienn
 ale was renamed Hãhãwpuá and curated by Arissana Pataxó\, Denilson Baniwa
 \, and Gustavo Caboco Wapichana. At its center was Glicéria Tupinambá's O
 kará Assojaba\, a work that grows out of her campaign for the Tupinambá m
 antles held in European collections. The pavilion displayed her letters t
 o seven European institutions and to Pope Francis\, asking that the mantl
 es be allowed to visit their people. That same year\, the mantle held in 
 Copenhagen returned to Brazil. Drawing on Eduardo Viveiros de Castro's "c
 ontrolled equivocation" and reading Glicéria's writings as Indigenous the
 ory\, I trace the gaps between her terms and those of museums and states.
  I argue that the mantle\, understood as a relative rather than an object
 \, unsettles both art history's comparative methods and the terms of offi
 cial repatriation.\n<p>Speaker\nCamila Maroja is an Assistant Professor o
 f Fine Arts at Brandeis University. She specializes in modern and contemp
 orary art within a Southern context. Her areas of interest include exhibi
 tion history\, localized experimental art practices since the 1960s\, art
  historiographies\, cultural exchanges between South and North\, cross-cu
 ltural methodologies\, the location of global art\, and the relationship 
 between Brazil and Latin America. She is the author of several articles a
 nd book chapters on 20th- and 21st-century art\, and her book project\, F
 raming Latin American Art\, examines how artists and critics in Brazil ha
 ve mobilized earlier tropes to construct something that could be named "L
 atin American." Her research has been supported by the National Endowment
  for the Humanities\, the Getty Research Foundation\, and the Cogut Cente
 r for International Humanities at Brown University.\n<p>We hope you can j
 oin us for this special event.\n<p>More info\, please contact:  Maria Pau
 la Molano Parrado (maria.molano@duke.edu) or Mateus Sanches Duarte (mateu
 s.duarte@duke.edu).
DURATION:PT1H30M
DTSTAMP:20261103T152604Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261109T153000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261103T152604Z
